WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.998 --> 00:00:03.992
[BIRDS CHIRPING]

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:11.860
I love running without headphones on.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.740
New York City always sounds different.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:16.450
I don't get bored.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:18.370
People having different conversations.

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:19.320
Excuse me.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.690
Different people walking different dogs.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.850
People that I see running and hear

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.350
the cadence of their footsteps.

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:30.110
Every so often, you come across a gutsy woman

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:31.880
like Melissa Blume.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.900
Born legally blind, Mel started running with Achilles in 2015

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.365
and has since completed five New York City marathons.

00:00:39.365 --> 00:00:39.865
Hi.

00:00:39.865 --> 00:00:40.350
Hi.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:40.850
Hi.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.230
I'm going to give you a hug because we

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.230
should have another hug.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:44.800
We definitely should.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:44.980
Yay.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:45.480
Hug it out.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:46.330
Hug it out.

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:47.470
I'm really bad about--

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:47.680
Freaking balance.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:48.222
--stretching.

00:00:48.222 --> 00:00:48.860
[LAUGHS]

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.620
Do a little stretch.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.880
I'm doing my right leg first.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:52.490
Oh, good Lord.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:52.990
All right.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:53.530
Oh.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:58.210
I mean, you have the one arm out and very statuesque.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.520
But you don't warm up or cool down?

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:01.270
Not really.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.860
And you've run almost 10 marathons?

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:04.360
Yeah.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.387
[LAUGHS]

00:01:05.387 --> 00:01:06.970
Basically, you're like, I'm a machine?

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:08.890
She's a total machine and won't admit it.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.360
Is this where you normally run?

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:10.960
Yes.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.200
And actually, we met in Central Park at an Achilles practice.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.490
Step down.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:16.600
1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:17.620
Step down.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:18.430
Down one more.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.180
Here we go.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.150
Achilles International is a global organization

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:25.580
that pairs athletes with disabilities

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.590
with able-bodied guides.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:30.230
Coming up, we've got a real dip.

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.000
Counting down.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.310
5, 4, 3, 2, dip.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.060
We met, I think, 2016.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:36.960
Nice and smooth.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.000
Keep your feet up.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.400
While I was training, I saw Achilles shirts.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.670
You could spot Achilles athletes in any crowd

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.040
with our bright neon yellow shirts.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.680
I saw athletes with several different disabilities,

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.940
athletes tethered.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.850
Line runners and you see athletes

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.100
with running blades and prosthetics.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.660
And I thought, oh, I wonder what that's about.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.110
So I researched online and applied.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.458
Just keep your feet up on the down.

00:02:03.458 --> 00:02:04.000
We'll cruise.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:09.729
Excuse us.

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:12.530
Come by me.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.920
Then follow me until we pass these people.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.600
My biggest challenge when running

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.680
is my visual impairment.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.050
So Brooke's there.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:21.840
She's my eyes.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:22.340
Careful.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:23.960
It's rolling in here, a little divot.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.580
OK.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:30.480
I have an eye condition that's called aniridia

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.910
that reduces sharpness in my vision

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.130
and makes my eyes very sensitive to light.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:40.440
I also have cataracts that make my vision blurry.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.990
A mutual friend of mine told me about Achilles.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:46.290
And ever since then, I've been coming almost every week.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:47.134
[LAUGHS]

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.700
I met Mel, I think, my first day at Achilles.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.940
We met and bonded over fashion that day,

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.560
and then we started running together.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.740
It's hard to run on your own with a visual impairment.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:01.420
Really, she's just been there for me.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:01.920
Sorry.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.170
Keep it right here.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.800
I run with Mel so much, it's second nature and the way

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.665
that we work and communicate.

00:03:09.665 --> 00:03:10.290
Plot foot here.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:11.430
Plot foot here.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:12.690
Uneven terrains--

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.010
With rocks and roots.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.720
It just took more communication.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.070
Root, rock, tree branch, ascending.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:19.350
We're starting to climb.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:20.640
So you narrate the whole time?

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.140
And she'll do it.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:23.070
She goes.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:23.708
Nice work, Mel.

00:03:23.708 --> 00:03:24.750
You don't even look down.

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:25.770
You just go.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:27.080
I like living on the edge.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:28.702
[LAUGHS]

00:03:28.702 --> 00:03:30.660
Has your family been able to come cheer you on?

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.940
They're definitely my biggest fans.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:36.598
And I really don't know where I would be--

00:03:36.598 --> 00:03:37.140
I don't know.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.690
I really don't know, to be honest, what my situation would

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.660
be like without them.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:48.840
Both of my parents are blind, and they found me.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.770
When I was three, I was adopted from Korea.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.810
They knew about my visual impairment.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.420
They've just been the best example for me.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.640
They taught me to really advocate for myself

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.673
and just let people know, it's not a bad thing.

00:04:02.673 --> 00:04:04.090
It's like, if you need extra help,

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:07.890
just let them know about your visual impairment, and it's OK.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.400
I could really just do anything, no limitations.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.880
That's just the way they raised me.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.910
No matter if it's going to be a good day or a bad day,

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:19.529
keep showing up for yourself.

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:20.188
That's awesome.

00:04:20.188 --> 00:04:20.980
How about yourself?

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.188
How did you get into running?

00:04:22.188 --> 00:04:24.240
So I got into running, really-- my dad ran.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.208
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:26.208 --> 00:04:29.160
I think you can run here forever and not get tired of it.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.740
I was like a kid running after my dad,

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.300
probably more accurately, than running with my dad.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.148
When I got to college, one of my friends

00:04:36.148 --> 00:04:37.690
was like, oh, you should try running.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.890
And I remember having to go buy a pair of running shoes.

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.090
I had never been on a run.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:44.520
And I ran 2 miles the first time.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.990
And I was like, I ran 2 miles.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:47.910
And one of my friends, who was on the Stanford tennis team,

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:48.930
was like, 2 miles?

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.730
And I was like, that was a big deal for me.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:51.930
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:57.480
And then I just ran more and more and just never stopped.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.100
I hear you're running the marathon.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.800
Well, I mean, I'm planning on it, yeah, because, I mean,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.600
the longest I've ever run is 11 miles.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:04.380
You'll get there.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:05.850
And all I want to do is finish.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:08.400
My daughter's so enthusiastic.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.860
And I feel, good Lord, such a responsibility to her now

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.420
to make her proud of me.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:14.040
So I can't disappoint her.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:15.450
That's what matters most to me.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:16.110
It'll be great.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.370
You're gutsy to her.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:22.770
Brooke and I have run all distances together, from a 5K

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:25.090
to a marathon to a 50k.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:25.590
Yeah.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:26.220
Are you done?

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.680
Do you think you'll do something beyond 50K?

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:29.640
What's next?

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:33.480
A 50-miler or I'm thinking about doing maybe a 100-miler

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:34.290
at some point.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:35.460
Completely nuts.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:36.950
[LAUGHS]

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:37.450
We'll see?

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:37.890
We'll see.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:38.390
TBD?

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:40.200
We'll think about it.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.920
We're running New York City Marathon too.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:42.420
All right.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:45.390
Well, I'll probably be cheering you on as you both pass me.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:46.590
I'll be cheering you on.

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:47.380
That's huge.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:48.165
I'll be waving.

00:05:48.165 --> 00:05:51.360
I'll be like, yay, I'll be back here, just trying to finish.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:52.920
[LAUGHS]

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.272
You'll get there.

00:05:54.272 --> 00:05:56.752
[CHEERING]

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.120
People give me all the credit.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:12.410
However, Brooke shows up for me because she wants to and not

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:15.440
because she has to, and that seems pretty gutsy to me.

